; expect 42
; Two expression forms stage0 compiles and stage1 refused, found by
; running every form the Rust parser accepts through both compilers and
; comparing the answers rather than by reading either one.
;
;   (- x)       negation. stage0 desugars it in the parser to `0 - x`.
;               stage1 did it in `emitCall` instead - at the far end of
;               the pipeline, past a type checker that met a
;               two-parameter builtin supplied with one argument and
;               reported **AX3013 partial-application on a valid
;               program**. Nothing ever reached the emitter's case, so
;               the case was both correct and dead. It is a parser
;               rewrite now, and the emitter's copy is gone.
;
;   (:: e T)    a type ascription in EXPRESSION position - the same
;               token as the top-level `(:: name T)` declaration and a
;               different form. Only the declaration was parsed, so an
;               ascribed expression reached `parseAppForm`, which asked
;               for an expression, found `::`, and answered a bare
;               `parse failed` with no span.
;
; The ascription was worse in the other compiler. stage0's parser and
; type checker both knew the form; its IR generator had no arm for it,
; so it fell through to the catch-all and `(:: 42 Int)` evaluated to
; **0** on a program `axiom check` had just called well typed. That is
; the shape `cond` had: implemented in every stage but the one that
; makes it run, and invisible because no `.ax` file in the repository
; ascribes an expression.
;
; `negated` and `ascribed` are separate functions rather than inline
; forms so that the negation crosses a call boundary, where a checker
; that had been given the answer by constant folding would not be.

(:: negated (-> Int Int))
(fn (negated n) (- n))

(:: ascribed (-> Int Int))
(fn (ascribed n) (:: n Int))

; A literal argument, which is the case stage0's formatter prints back
; as `-8` rather than as a call.
(:: literalNegation Int)
(fn (literalNegation) (- 8))

; `(- 0 (negated 4))` rather than `(- 0 (- 4))`, which is a different
; bug in a different compiler and has its own case: stage0 prints the
; inner negation back as the literal `-4`, and `-4` in an argument
; position re-lexes as two tokens, so the form grows a third argument on
; the next pass. `fmt` catches that as a lost fixed point and refuses.
(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (+ (ascribed 50)
     (+ (negated 4) (+ (literalNegation) (- 0 (negated 4))))))
